-- OCC-001: task_occurrences realtime DELETE events generated by recurrence
-- regeneration were silently dropped for the app's filtered subscription
-- (`.eq('household_id', ...)` combined with postgres_changes DELETE
-- tracking) — the same mechanism proven and fixed for shopping_items
-- (20260922000001), tasks (20260922000002), and expenses (20260922000003).
--
-- task_occurrences' only RLS policy (task_occurrences_select_active_member,
-- using is_active_household_member(household_id)) and the client's
-- household_id filter both need household_id — a non-primary-key column —
-- present in the DELETE change payload to decide whether the event is
-- visible to a given subscriber. Without full replica identity, that
-- column isn't included and Realtime drops the event for that subscriber,
-- even though the DELETE itself (issued by _generate_occurrences_for_task
-- during recurrence/due-date regeneration) commits successfully.
--
-- Physically reproduced: editing a task's due date from one future
-- occurrence to another left the obsolete occurrence stale in the active
-- Tasks-screen subscription (INSERT of the replacement arrived; DELETE of
-- the obsolete row did not), while a fresh subscription showed the correct
-- state immediately.
--
-- Scoped to task_occurrences only. expense_settlements carries the same
-- theoretical risk but remains BLOCKED — safe reproduction not available —
-- and is left untouched here.
alter table task_occurrences replica identity full;
